  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 915 PM MDT Wed May 20 2026\n\nWe continue to be situated under an upper level trough that will \nslowly begin to track eastward by the end of the week, giving way to \na ridge of high pressure. In the meantime, temperatures will hover \nat or below normal for the next few days, before warming to above \nnormal by Sunday. In terms of precipitation, Thursday afternoon will \nsee the best chance (50-70%) for scattered showers with some \nthunderstorms possible across the northern part of our CWA. \nAlthough, accumulations appear to be quite minimal. As the shortwave \ntrough and associated cold front push through the area into Thursday \nevening, precipitation chances have the ability to spread further \nsouth along the I-70 corridor and central mountains. Additionally, \nas a result of this system moving through, gusty winds will be \npresent. Gusts will generally fall within the 25-30 mph range, with \nlocally higher gusts possible. Critical fire weather conditions will \nexist for tomorrow afternoon, refer to the fire weather section for \nmore on that. Friday will be mostly dry following the frontal \npassage with a chance (30%) for light showers in the northern \nmountains due to lingering moisture. \n\nTHE WEEKEND AND BEYOND: \n\nAs we move into Memorial Day weekend, conditions appear to be quite \npleasant as the aforementioned ridge of high pressure begins to \nsituate itself over the Intermountain West. As previously mentioned, \ntemperatures will be about 5-10 degrees above normal with this \nridge. Due to some lingering energy and available moisture, \nafternoon convection could be supported each day through the \nweekend, favoring the higher terrain. Beyond Sunday, another low \npressure system will establish itself over the Pacific Northwest \ngiving way to increased precipitation chances for our area Monday \ninto Tuesday.",
